\section*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
This report documents the design, implementation, and testing of the Wolf Pack Survival Adventure Game, a text-based survival game built using C++17 and Qt6. The project demonstrates practical applications of fundamental data structures including binary decision trees, priority queues, linked lists, stacks, and queues in a gaming context. Players assume the role of a lone wolf separated from their pack during a harsh winter storm, tasked with surviving 30 days in the wilderness through strategic decision-making, resource management, and pack building. The game features 30+ decision nodes, 23 unique endings across three storylines, and fully integrated GUI and terminal interfaces with difficulty-scaled item drops.

\textbf{Keywords:} Data Structures, Binary Trees, Priority Queues, Linked Lists, Game Development, C++, Qt6, Wolf Survival, Difficulty Scaling

\newpage

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Project Overview}
The Wolf Pack Survival Adventure Game is an interactive text-based survival game developed as the final project for the Data Structures Lab course. The game places players in the role of a young wolf separated from their pack during a harsh winter storm. The objective is to survive for 30 days in the wilderness by making critical decisions, managing resources, building alliances, and establishing or finding a new pack.

This project was chosen for its creative potential and the unique opportunity it provides to demonstrate how data structures power game mechanics, AI decision-making, and narrative branching. Unlike typical business applications, this game shows data structures in a gaming context that is both engaging and educational. The project combines logic with creativity, making it fun to build and test while meeting all course requirements.

The development spanned one week with a team structure that distributed responsibilities across story design, character management, combat systems, and GUI implementation. The final product exceeds minimum requirements with 30+ decision nodes (vs. 20 required), 23 endings (vs. 5 required), and full integration of all six required data structures including a difficulty-scaled inventory system.

\subsection{Learning Objectives}
By completing this project, students gain hands-on experience with:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Implementing binary trees for decision-based narratives and branching storylines
	\item Using priority queues for threat management and event scheduling
	\item Applying stacks for undo functionality and game state history
	\item Implementing linked lists for dynamic inventory systems
	\item Using queues for action sequences and multi-turn activities
	\item Creating game AI using data structures for event probability
	\item Building interactive GUI for storytelling with Qt6
	\item Understanding game state management including save/load functionality
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Project Structure}
The project is organized into the following directory structure:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \texttt{src/} - Source files (.cpp)
	\item \texttt{include/} - Header files (.h)
	\item \texttt{build/} - Build output directory
	\item \texttt{wolf\_pack\_survival\_report.tex} - LaTeX report
	\item \texttt{CMakeLists.txt} - CMake build configuration
\end{itemize}

Build instructions: navigate to the build directory, run \texttt{cmake ..}, then \texttt{make}. The project produces three executables: WolfGame (main game), Validator (decision tree validation), and BalanceTester (game balance testing).

\subsection{Report Structure}
This report is organized according to the requirements specified in TeamA.txt. Section 2 presents the Game Design Document including story overview, game mechanics, and win/lose conditions. Section 3 describes the System Architecture with detailed explanations of how data structures are used. Section 4 provides the Data Structure Implementation with code examples and complexity analysis. Section 5 presents the Story Flowchart with a visual representation of the decision tree. Section 6 covers Testing and Game Balance results. Section 7 documents the use of AI tools in development. Section 8 provides the conclusion and future improvements.

\newpage

\section{Game Design Document}

\subsection{Story Overview and Setting}
The game begins with the player character, a young wolf, separated from their pack during a violent winter storm. The wolf awakens disoriented in a snow-covered wilderness, alone and hungry. The pack is nowhere to be seen, and survival becomes the immediate priority. The player must navigate through a dangerous world filled with threats, opportunities, and other wolves who may become allies or rivals.

The narrative is designed to be immersive and realistic to wolf behavior. Every decision reflects the harsh realities of survival in the wild: hunting or scavenging, fighting or fleeing, building relationships or maintaining independence. The story creates meaningful branching paths where each decision has consequences that ripple through the gameplay experience.

The game world is structured around a 30-day survival challenge. Each in-game day represents a series of decisions and actions that the player must manage carefully. The passage of time brings both opportunities and dangers. Players must balance immediate survival needs with long-term goals like building a pack or establishing territory.

The story features multiple narrative threads that intersect based on player choices. Some paths lead to forming a powerful pack and becoming the alpha. Others result in finding the original pack after a long journey. Some endings are tragic - the wolf may starve, freeze, or fall to predators. The richness of the narrative comes from the player's agency in determining which story unfolds.

\subsection{Game Mechanics}
The game operates on a core loop of decision-making and resource management. At each decision point, the player is presented with a scenario description and two choices (labeled A and B). Each choice leads to a new scenario in the decision tree and affects the player's statistics.

Statistics are the numerical representation of the wolf's condition and capabilities:

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\caption{Wolf Statistics}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{8cm}c@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Statistic} & \textbf{Description}                                            & \textbf{Range} \\ \midrule
		Health             & Physical condition; decreases with injuries, hunger, and combat & 0-100          \\
		Hunger             & Satiety level; increases with time and effort, death at 100     & 0-100          \\
		Energy             & Physical and mental stamina; required for choices and actions   & 0-100          \\
		Reputation         & Social standing; affects pack recruitment and NPC interactions  & 0-100          \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\vspace{0.3cm}

Statistics are interconnected in their effects. Hunger increases by a base amount of 5 points per decision or action. If hunger exceeds 80, health begins to decrease due to starvation effects. Energy is consumed by actions and restored through rest or pack benefits. Choices have energy requirements - attempting an action without sufficient energy results in an error message.

The difficulty system scales the challenge by adjusting hunger increase rates and event probabilities:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Easy mode: adds 3 hunger per action with 20\% event chance
	\item Normal mode: adds 5 hunger with 30\% event chance
	\item Hard mode: adds 8 hunger with 50\% event chance
\end{itemize}

Pack membership adds complexity to resource management. When the player has pack members, hunger increases faster (by 10\% per pack member) because more mouths must be fed. However, pack members provide benefits: hunters increase food acquisition chances, scouts provide early warning of threats, and guards offer protection in combat situations.

The inventory system provides strategic depth beyond statistics. Players collect items through exploration, events, and scenario rewards. The inventory has a maximum capacity of 10 items, forcing players to make choices about what to carry.

\textbf{Inventory Item Types:}

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\caption{Inventory Item Categories}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{8cm}c@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Examples}                                      & \textbf{Effect}  \\ \midrule
		FOOD          & Small Fish, Winter Berries, Bird Egg, Insects, Frog    & Reduces Hunger   \\
		WATER         & Fresh Water                                            & Thirst Relief    \\
		HEALING       & Common Mallow, Root Paste, Moss Dressing, Bee Propolis & Restores Health  \\
		TOOLS         & Hunting Trap, Sharp Rock                               & Special Benefits \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Difficulty-Scaled Item Drops:} The inventory system includes difficulty-scaled methods that adjust item quantities based on the selected difficulty:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{addStartingSupplies()} - Adds starting items based on difficulty:
	      \begin{itemize}
		      \item Easy: Small Fish x2, Winter Berries x2, Common Mallow x1
		      \item Normal: No starting items
		      \item Hard: Insects x2
	      \end{itemize}
	\item \textbf{addRandomFood()} - Random food drops with difficulty scaling
	\item \textbf{addRandomHealing()} - Random healing drops with difficulty scaling
	\item \textbf{addScavengedItems()} - Mixed scavenged items based on difficulty
	\item \textbf{addHuntLoot()} - Scenario-specific loot with difficulty modifiers
\end{itemize}

The quantity scaling ensures Easy mode provides more abundant resources (3 food, 2 healing, 3 scavenged), while Hard mode offers scarce resources (1 food, 0 healing, 1 scavenged).

The event system introduces randomness and urgency. Random events occur based on a probability check after each decision. Critical events (priority 1) like bear attacks require immediate attention. Urgent events (priority 2) like severe hunger need prompt action. Normal events (priority 3) like weather changes provide information but affect planning. The "Found Berries" event now uses addRandomFood() with difficulty scaling and provides Fresh Water based on difficulty level.

Win and lose conditions determine the game's endpoint. Victory is achieved by surviving 30 days, with the specific ending determined by the player's choices and statistics throughout the game. Defeat occurs if health reaches 0 (death) or hunger reaches 100 (starvation).

\subsection{Multiple Endings}
The game features 23 unique endings across three storylines, organized into five major categories:

\textbf{Legendary Alpha Ending:} Achieved by building a large, loyal pack (5+ members) with high reputation (80+). The wolf becomes the dominant force in the territory, respected by all and feared by rivals.

\textbf{Peaceful Leader Ending:} Achieved through wisdom and diplomacy rather than strength. The wolf forms alliances, avoids unnecessary conflicts, and builds a harmonious pack.

\textbf{Tragic Hero Ending:} Occurs when the wolf makes noble sacrifices for others but ultimately falls. This might involve defending pack members at personal cost or sharing resources until nothing remains.

\textbf{Diplomatic Alpha Ending:} Achieved by uniting multiple packs or factions through negotiation. The wolf transcends individual pack loyalty to create a larger coalition.

\textbf{Lone Wanderer Ending:} Achieved by rejecting pack formation and surviving alone. While possible, this ending is the most challenging as players forego pack benefits.

Each ending category contains multiple variations based on specific choices and statistics. This variety ensures replayability and rewards multiple playthroughs.

\subsection{User Interface Design}
The game offers two interface options: terminal mode for text-based gameplay and GUI mode using Qt6 for a graphical experience.

Terminal mode presents a classic text adventure experience with ASCII art representations of scenarios, color-coded statistics output, and keyboard-based input.

GUI mode provides a modern, visual interface with:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Top Section - Status Panel:} Animated progress bars for stats, day counter, pack size
	\item \textbf{Middle Section - Story Display:} Scrollable text area for narrative descriptions
	\item \textbf{Choice Section - Decision Buttons:} Two buttons for A and B choices
	\item \textbf{Bottom Section - Game Controls:} Menu bar, undo, save/load, inventory, pack buttons
\end{itemize}

Both modes include save/load functionality with three slots. Auto-save occurs every 5 decisions. The undo feature allows players to reverse their last decision (up to 5 levels deep).

