\section{System Architecture}

\subsection{Overall System Design}
The Wolf Pack Survival Game follows a modular architecture that separates concerns into distinct components: game logic, data structures, user interface, and persistence. This design allows for independent development and testing of each module while ensuring smooth integration during gameplay.

The core game engine handles the main game loop: displaying the current scenario, accepting player input, updating game state based on choices, processing events, and checking win/lose conditions. This loop runs continuously until the player quits, wins, or dies. The game state includes the current decision tree node, wolf statistics, inventory, pack, day counter, and event queue.

Data structures serve as the foundation for game mechanics:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Binary decision tree drives narrative progression
	\item Priority queue manages threat events
	\item Linked lists handle inventory and pack storage
	\item Stack implements undo functionality
	\item Queue sequences multi-turn actions
\end{itemize}

The user interface layer abstracts game logic from presentation. Both terminal and GUI modes use the same underlying game engine, simply providing different views and input methods.

Persistence is handled through save/load functionality that serializes the complete game state to text files. This includes all statistics, the current tree node ID, inventory items, pack members, and game progress.

\textbf{Figure 2: System Architecture Diagram}
\begin{center}
	\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=1.5cm, every node/.style={rectangle,draw,align=center,minimum width=3cm}]
		\node (user) [fill=lightblue, minimum width=4cm] {User Input\\(Terminal/GUI)};
		\node (interface) [below=of user, fill=yellow!50] {User Interface Layer\\(Input Handling, Display)};
		\node (engine) [below=of interface, fill=green!50] {Game Engine\\Main Loop, State Management};
		\node (structures) [below=of engine, fill=orange!50] {Data Structures\\Tree, PQ, Lists, Stack, Queue};
		\node (persistence) [below=of structures, fill=purple!50] {Persistence\\Save/Load Files};

		\draw[->,thick] (user) -- (interface);
		\draw[->,thick] (interface) -- (engine);
		\draw[->,thick] (engine) -- (structures);
		\draw[->,thick] (engine) -- (persistence);
		\draw[<->,thick,dashed] (structures) -- (persistence);
	\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Source: Author's creation}

The game initialization process sets up all components before the main loop begins. The decision tree is built based on the selected storyline (Classic, Survival, or Pack). Statistics are initialized based on difficulty and storyline. The event queue is populated with potential threats. The history stack starts empty. The player begins at the root node of the decision tree.

During gameplay, each decision triggers a chain of operations: push current state to history, navigate tree to child node, update statistics, process random events, calculate pack benefits, update display, continue loop.

Game state changes are atomic - either the entire decision process completes or nothing changes. This prevents partial updates that could corrupt game state.

The game handles various error conditions gracefully. Invalid save files result in error messages without crashing. Null decision nodes trigger fallback paths with graceful degradation. Out-of-memory situations are handled through exception catching and user notification.

\subsection{Difficulty-Scaled Inventory System}
A key enhancement to the game is the difficulty-scaled inventory system that adjusts resource availability based on the selected difficulty level. This system ensures that Easy mode provides a more forgiving experience while Hard mode offers a genuine survival challenge.

The inventory system is implemented using a linked list structure with methods that accept a Difficulty parameter to scale item quantities:

\textbf{Inventory Methods with Difficulty Scaling:}

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\caption{Difficulty-Scaled Inventory Methods}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{6cm}c@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Method}       & \textbf{Purpose}                  & \textbf{Parameters} \\ \midrule
		addStartingSupplies() & Adds initial items at game start  & Difficulty enum     \\
		addRandomFood()       & Generates random food drops       & Difficulty enum     \\
		addRandomHealing()    & Generates random healing items    & Difficulty enum     \\
		addScavengedItems()   & Creates scavenged item bundles    & Difficulty enum     \\
		addHuntLoot()         & Scenario-specific hunting rewards & Difficulty, int     \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Quantity Scaling by Difficulty:}

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\caption{Item Quantity Scaling by Difficulty}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Item Type}   & \textbf{Easy} & \textbf{Normal} & \textbf{Hard} \\ \midrule
		Food Items           & 3             & 1               & 1             \\
		Healing Items        & 2             & 1               & 0             \\
		Scavenged Items      & 3             & 1               & 1             \\
		Starting Supplies    & Full kit      & None            & Minimal       \\
		Fresh Water (Events) & 2             & 1               & 1             \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\vspace{0.3cm}

The system is integrated into:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Game initialization (addStartingSupplies called at game start)
	\item Random events (Found Berries event uses addRandomFood)
	\item Scenario outcomes (scavenging and hunting scenarios)
	\item Both terminal and GUI interfaces
\end{itemize}

This scaling mechanism is implemented in inventory.cpp with O(1) complexity for item addition, ensuring no performance impact during gameplay.

\subsection{Decision Tree Structure}
The decision tree is the core narrative driver. Each node represents a story scenario with two possible choices (A and B), creating a binary branching structure. Leaf nodes represent story endings.

The DecisionNode struct contains:
\begin{itemize}
	\item scenarioID: Unique identifier
	\item description: Narrative text
	\item choiceA\_text, choiceB\_text: Option texts
	\item left, right: Child node pointers
	\item isEnding: Boolean for terminal nodes
	\item endingText: Victory/defeat message
\end{itemize}

The tree supports three storylines:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Classic Story:} Balanced gameplay with standard starting stats
	\item \textbf{Survival Story:} Challenging mode with lower starting health (40), higher hunger (80)
	\item \textbf{Pack Story:} Leadership-focused with reputation bonus (20) and pre-recruited pack members
\end{itemize}

The tree has 30+ total nodes across all storylines with 23 unique paths to endings. Navigation is O(1) per step with direct child pointers. Tree construction is O(n) for n nodes. The Classic storyline includes additional scavenging scenarios (nodes 40-42) that provide difficulty-scaled item rewards.

\textbf{Figure 3: Simplified Decision Tree Structure}
\begin{center}
	\begin{tikzpicture}[level distance=2cm, sibling distance=4cm, every node/.style={rectangle,draw,align=center,minimum width=1.5cm}]
		\node {1: Start\\(Lost Wolf)}
		child {node {2: Hunt\\(Follow Scent)}
				child {node {4: Track\\(Deer)}}
				child {node {5: Wait\\(Howl)}}
			}
		child {node {3: Shelter\\(Find Cave)}}
		;
	\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Source: Author's creation, inspired by TeamA.txt tree structure}

Safety features include null pointer checks, fallback nodes for unexpected paths, and the validator tool confirming tree integrity.

\subsection{Priority Queue for Events}
The priority queue implements a min-heap for managing game events by urgency. Events are inserted with priority 1-3 (lower = more urgent), and the queue returns the highest-priority event first.

The Event struct contains:
\begin{itemize}
	\item name: Event identifier (e.g., "Bear Attack")
	\item priority: Integer 1-3
	\item description: Text displayed to player
	\item effect: Lambda function modifying wolf statistics
\end{itemize}

Event categories:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Priority 1 (Critical):} Bear attack, hunter nearby, severe injury
	\item \textbf{Priority 2 (Urgent):} Severe hunger, infection, rival pack sighting
	\item \textbf{Priority 3 (Normal):} Weather change, prey spotted, lone wolf encounter
\end{itemize}

Heap operations: insert() O(log n), extractMin() O(log n), isEmpty() O(1).

\textbf{Table 2: Event Examples by Priority}
\begin{center}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}cllc@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Priority} & \textbf{Event}   & \textbf{Effect}                   & \textbf{Probability}  \\ \midrule
		1                 & Bear Attack      & Health -20, Energy -10            & Low (event chance)    \\
		1                 & Hunter Nearby    & Spirit -20, Reputation -10        & Low (event chance)    \\
		2                 & Severe Hunger    & Health -10, Hunger +10            & Medium (event chance) \\
		2                 & Injury Worsening & Health -15, Energy -10            & Medium (event chance) \\
		3                 & Weather Change   & Energy -5, Hunger +5              & High (event chance)   \\
		3                 & Found Berries    & Hunger -10, +Food items (scaled)  & High (event chance)   \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}

The difficulty setting affects event probability (Easy: 20\%, Normal: 30\%, Hard: 50\%). Additionally, the Found Berries event now applies difficulty-scaled item drops using inventory.addRandomFood(difficulty), providing more food items on Easy mode and Fresh Water (2x on Easy, 1x on Normal/Hard).

\textbf{Figure 4: Priority Queue Event Processing Flow}
\begin{center}
	\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=1.5cm, every node/.style={rectangle,draw,align=center,minimum width=3cm}]
		\node (decision) [fill=lightblue] {Player Makes\\Decision};
		\node (randomcheck) [below=of decision, fill=yellow!50] {Random Check\\(< eventChance)?};
		\node (extract) [below=of randomcheck, fill=orange!50] {extractMin()\\Highest Priority Event};
		\node (apply) [below=of extract, fill=green!50] {Apply Event\\Effect to Stats};
		\node (reinsert) [below=of apply, fill=purple!50] {Reinsert Event\\to Queue};
		\node (continue) [below=of reinsert, fill=lightgray] {Continue\\Game Loop};

		\draw[->,thick] (decision) -- (randomcheck);
		\draw[->,thick] (randomcheck) -- node[right] {Yes} (extract);
		\draw[->,thick] (randomcheck) -- node[left] {No} (continue);
		\draw[->,thick] (extract) -- (apply);
		\draw[->,thick] (apply) -- (reinsert);
		\draw[->,thick] (reinsert) -- (continue);
	\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Source: Author's creation}

Time-based events trigger at specific intervals (every 5 days for food consumption).

\subsection{Game State Stack for Undo}
The game state stack implements LIFO behavior to provide undo functionality. Before each decision, the current complete game state is pushed onto the stack.

The GameState struct captures:
\begin{itemize}
	\item currentNode: Pointer to decision tree node
	\item health, hunger, energy: Wolf statistics
	\item day: Current day counter
	\item inventory: Pointer to cloned inventory state
\end{itemize}

Operations: push() O(1), pop() O(1), isEmpty() O(1). Maximum depth of 5 levels prevents excessive memory usage.

Inventory cloning is critical - a deep copy of the linked list is created when saving state. When undoing, the current inventory is replaced with the cloned version.

\textbf{Figure 5: Stack Operations for Undo}
\begin{center}
	\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=1cm, every node/.style={rectangle,draw,align=center,minimum width=4cm}]
		\node (state0) [fill=lightgray!30] {Initial State};
		\node (state1) [below=of state0, fill=yellow!30] {State 1\\(After Choice A)};
		\node (state2) [below=of state1, fill=orange!30] {State 2\\(After Choice B)};
		\node (state3) [below=of state2, fill=green!30] {State 3\\(After Choice A)};

		\draw[->,thick,dashed] ([xshift=-0.5cm] state0.south) -- ([xshift=-0.5cm] state1.north);
		\draw[->,thick,dashed] ([xshift=-0.5cm] state1.south) -- ([xshift=-0.5cm] state2.north);
		\draw[->,thick,dashed] ([xshift=-0.5cm] state2.south) -- ([xshift=-0.5cm] state3.north);

		\node (top) [left=of state3, fill=red!20, minimum width=1cm] {TOP};
		\draw[->,thick] (top) -- (state3);

		\node (bottom) [left=of state0, fill=blue!20, minimum width=1cm] {BOTTOM};
		\draw[->,thick] (bottom) -- (state0);
	\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Source: Author's creation}

Memory management requires careful attention - each pushed state includes a dynamically allocated inventory clone that must be deleted when popped.

The action queue complements the stack by handling sequential multi-turn activities using FIFO ordering. When a player chooses a complex action like hunting a deer, the action is broken into steps that process in subsequent turns.

Complexity analysis confirms O(1) push/pop operations. Inventory cloning is O(n) where n is the number of items (max 10). The fixed maximum depth of 5 ensures bounded memory usage.

Edge cases handled: undo after save/load (empty stack), undo at maximum depth, undo after game over, and invalid state detection from corrupted save files.

This implementation demonstrates practical application of stack data structure principles in a real-world gaming context, providing essential undo functionality while maintaining performance and stability.

\section{5. Ending Verification and Storyline Analysis}

\subsection{5.1 Validator Tool Overview}
A dedicated Validator tool was developed to verify the decision tree structure and ensure all endings are reachable. The Validator performs the following checks:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Counts total unique nodes to verify 20+ requirement
	\item Counts total endings to verify 5+ requirement
	\item Traces all possible paths from root to endings
	\item Reports all ending IDs for completeness verification
\end{itemize}

The Validator was enhanced to test all three storylines (Classic, Survival, Pack) independently, ensuring each meets project requirements.

